  quiz: [
    {q:"Under Utah law, at what point must an insurable interest exist for a life insurance policy?", c:["At the time of the insured's death","At the policy's effective date","Both at issue and at the time of death","Only when the beneficiary files a claim"], a:1, w:"31A-21-104(2)(c) requires insurable interest at the policy's effective date (and when any later interest in the proceeds is acquired) and says it need not exist when proceeds become payable. 'Both at issue and at death' is the tempting wrong answer; that's a property insurance idea.", o:"3.1", ut:true},
    {q:"Rick bought a $250,000 policy on his wife Dana's life, with her written consent, and named himself beneficiary. They divorced three years later, Rick kept paying premiums, and Dana has now died. What happens?", c:["Rick collects, because insurable interest existed when the policy was issued","The policy is void because Rick lost his insurable interest at the divorce","The insurer refunds premiums to Rick","Proceeds go to Dana's estate automatically"], a:0, w:"In Utah insurable interest is tested at the effective date of the policy, not at death, so a divorce doesn't destroy the policy. The 'void at divorce' answer applies the wrong timing rule.", o:"3.1", ut:true},
    {q:"In Utah, each of the following may insure another person's life WITHOUT that person's consent EXCEPT:", c:["A parent insuring a 6-year-old child","A creditor insuring a debtor, at the creditor's expense, for an amount related to the debt","A business insuring its 40-year-old sales manager","A person insuring an immediate family member who lives with and depends on them"], a:2, w:"Employer-owned coverage on directors, officers, managers or employees requires the insured's written consent (31A-21-104(3)(d)). The other three are express no-consent exceptions in 31A-21-104(5)(a).", o:"3.1", ut:true},
    {q:"An investor offers a 78-year-old a cash payment to apply for a large policy that the investor's trust will fund and later take over. Under Utah law this arrangement is:", c:["A legal life settlement, because the insured consented","A viatical settlement, which Utah permits for anyone over 65","Stranger-originated life insurance, which violates Utah's insurable interest law","Allowed if the policy is held at least 15 days"], a:2, w:"Initiating a policy for an investor with no insurable interest, with an agreement at issue to transfer it, is STOLI (31A-36-102(18)) and violates 31A-21-104. A life settlement is the later sale of a policy that was validly issued; consent alone doesn't create insurable interest.", o:"3.3", ut:true},
    {q:"A Utah owner sells her policy to a life settlement provider and receives the proceeds on March 1. Until when may she rescind the settlement?", c:["March 11","March 31","She cannot rescind once proceeds are paid","March 16"], a:3, w:"Utah's rescission period ends 15 calendar days after the owner receives the proceeds (31A-36-109), so March 16. Ten days is the life insurance free look, a different rule.", o:"3.3", ut:true},
    {q:"Which of these is NOT a life settlement under the Utah Life Settlements Act?", c:["An accelerated death benefit paid by the insurer under the policy's terms","A sale of a policy by a terminally ill owner, called a viatical settlement","A sale of a policy by a healthy 80-year-old, called a senior settlement","A premium finance loan where the owner agrees at the start to sell the policy later"], a:0, w:"31A-36-102(8)(d) excludes a loan or accelerated death benefit paid by the insurer. Utah treats viatical and senior settlements as life settlements whatever they're called, and a premium-finance loan paired with an agreement to sell is pulled in too.", o:"3.3", ut:true},
    {q:"An owner wants to settle a policy issued 14 months ago. Utah permits it only if the owner certifies an exception. Which situation qualifies?", c:["The owner wants cash for a vacation home","The owner's spouse has died","The policy's cash value has grown faster than expected","The provider offers more than the cash surrender value"], a:1, w:"Within 2 years of issue, a settlement needs an exception such as the spouse's death, divorce, retirement, terminal or chronic illness, disability, bankruptcy or a major unexpected loss of income (31A-36-111(2)). A better price or a wish for cash isn't an exception.", o:"3.3", ut:true},
    {q:"A producer calculates insurance by totaling the family's final expenses, mortgage payoff, college fund and income needs through the Social Security blackout period, then subtracting existing assets. This is the:", c:["Human life value approach","Capital retention approach only","Needs approach","Replacement ratio method"], a:2, w:"Adding up specific family needs and subtracting resources is the needs approach. Human life value instead discounts the insured's future earnings. Capital retention is a variation of the needs approach that preserves principal, not the name of the whole method.", o:"3.4", ut:false},
    {q:"Which personal use of life insurance best fits a wealthy 70-year-old whose estate is mostly a family ranch?", c:["Estate creation","Estate conservation, providing liquidity to pay taxes and costs without selling the ranch","Juvenile savings","Income replacement for young children"], a:1, w:"Life insurance gives heirs cash for taxes and settlement costs so they don't have to sell illiquid assets. Estate creation fits a young person with few assets who needs to create an estate instantly.", o:"3.2", ut:false},
    {q:"Four equal partners want a cross-purchase buy-sell agreement funded with life insurance. How many policies are needed?", c:["4","8","16","12"], a:3, w:"Cross-purchase requires each owner to insure every other owner: n × (n − 1) = 4 × 3 = 12. Four policies would be an entity (stock redemption) plan, where the business owns one policy on each partner.", o:"3.5", ut:false},
    {q:"An employer pays the premium on a policy owned by its vice president and treats the payment as additional compensation. Which statement is correct?", c:["The employer deducts the premium and the VP reports it as taxable income","Neither party has any tax consequence","The employer cannot deduct it, and the VP receives it tax-free","This is a key person plan"], a:0, w:"That's a Section 162 executive bonus plan: the bonus is deductible compensation to the employer and taxable income to the employee. In key person coverage the business owns the policy, is beneficiary, and can't deduct the premium.", o:"3.5", ut:false},
    {q:"A policy sold door to door with a small face amount and weekly premiums collected by a debit agent is:", c:["Group life","Credit life","Industrial (home service) life","Ordinary life"], a:2, w:"Industrial or home service life is defined by small face amounts and frequent premiums collected at the home. Ordinary life is the larger, individually underwritten class with annual to monthly premiums.", o:"3.6", ut:false},
    {q:"Under Utah law, which insurer may issue ONLY participating life policies (aside from narrow exceptions)?", c:["A stock insurer","A fraternal benefit society","A mutual insurer","A reciprocal exchange"], a:1, w:"31A-22-418 lets both stock and mutual insurers issue participating and nonparticipating policies, but limits fraternals to participating policies with narrow exceptions. 'Mutual' is tempting because mutuals are traditionally par, but Utah doesn't restrict them.", o:"3.6", ut:true},
    {q:"All other factors equal, which change would LOWER a life insurer's premium rates?", c:["Higher assumed interest earnings","Higher expected mortality","Higher commission costs","Paying premiums monthly instead of annually"], a:0, w:"Interest earned on premiums offsets cost, so a higher assumed interest rate lowers the premium. Higher mortality and higher expenses raise it, and monthly mode costs more in total than annual.", o:"3.7", ut:false},
    {q:"An applicant gave a conditional receipt and the first premium on May 1, had a medical exam on May 5, and was killed in an accident May 9. The insurer later finds she was insurable as applied for. The insurer will:", c:["Refund the premium only, since the policy wasn't issued","Pay the claim, with coverage effective May 5","Pay the claim, with coverage effective May 9","Deny the claim because the policy wasn't delivered"], a:1, w:"A conditional receipt makes coverage effective on the later of the application or the exam (here May 5) if the applicant is found insurable as applied for. Issue and delivery aren't required; insurability is the condition.", o:"3.8", ut:false},
    {q:"A Utah applicant's answers show the new policy will replace an existing one. By when must the producer give the applicant the Utah replacement notice?", c:["Within 10 days after delivery","At the time of policy delivery","Within 5 business days after the application","No later than the time the application is taken"], a:3, w:"31A-22-429(2) requires the replacement notice no later than when the application is taken. Sales material, by contrast, must be left no later than delivery, and 5 business days is the replacing insurer's deadline to notify the existing insurer (R590-93).", o:"3.8", ut:true},
    {q:"After the application is signed, a producer notices the applicant gave the wrong birth date. Under Utah law, the correct way to fix it is:", c:["The producer corrects the date and initials it","The applicant corrects it (initialing the change) or signs a new application","The underwriter corrects it at the home office","The error doesn't matter because misstatement of age is adjusted at death"], a:1, w:"31A-21-105(1)(a) lets only the applicant, or someone with the applicant's written consent, alter the application except for purely administrative changes. A producer changing answers alone is not allowed.", o:"3.8", ut:true},
    {q:"A Utah life insurer learns an applicant misstated her weight. The insurer may avoid the policy only if:", c:["The misstatement was in any way inaccurate","The producer failed to verify it","It relied on the statement and it was material or made with intent to deceive, or the fact contributed to the loss","The insured dies within 5 years"], a:2, w:"Under 31A-21-105(2) a misrepresentation matters only if the insurer relied on it and it was material or intended to deceive, or the misstated fact contributed to the loss. A trivial inaccuracy isn't enough. (After 2 years the incontestable clause also limits contests.)", o:"3.9", ut:true},
    {q:"Which source of underwriting information is the 'field underwriting' done by the producer?", c:["The Medical Information Bureau report","The attending physician's statement","The producer's report on the application","The investigative consumer report"], a:2, w:"The producer is the first underwriter, recording observations in the producer's (agent's) report. MIB, physician statements and consumer reports are gathered by the home office.", o:"3.9", ut:false}
  ]
